---
import PageTitle from "@/components/PageTitle.astro";
import PageLayout from "@/layouts/Base.astro";
import "@/styles/explore.css";

const meta = {
	description:
		"An interactive galaxy luminosity function from the PAU Survey. Choose a band and a redshift slice, and see what photometric-redshift outliers do to the bright end.",
	title: "Counting galaxies",
	ogImage: "/images/PCA.webp",
};
---

<PageLayout meta={meta}>
	<PageTitle
		title="Counting galaxies"
		subtitle="How many galaxies of each brightness does the Universe contain, and how has that changed?"
	/>

	<p class="explore-status" data-status hidden></p>

	<div class="demo-lf" data-demo>
		{/*
			Controls come first: this demo is a comparison, and the reader needs to
			see what they can change before they look at the plot. Buttons are
			written by the script from lf.json, so a band or a slice can never be
			offered that the data does not have.
		*/}
		<div class="demo-chips">
			<div class="demo-chips__row">
				<span class="demo-chips__label" id="lf-band-label">Band</span>
				<div class="demo-chips__set" data-chips="band" role="group" aria-labelledby="lf-band-label"></div>
			</div>
			<div class="demo-chips__row">
				<span class="demo-chips__label" id="lf-z-label">Redshift</span>
				<div class="demo-chips__set" data-chips="z" role="group" aria-labelledby="lf-z-label"></div>
			</div>
			<div class="demo-chips__row">
				<span class="demo-chips__label" id="lf-pz-label">Photo-<i>z</i> errors</span>
				<div class="demo-chips__set" data-chips="pz" role="group" aria-labelledby="lf-pz-label"></div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="demo-chart demo-chart--lf">
			<canvas data-canvas role="img"
				aria-label="Galaxy luminosity function: number density of galaxies against absolute magnitude, comparing the PAU Survey measurement with the GALFORM lightcone."
			></canvas>
			<div class="demo-legend" data-legend></div>
		</div>

		<p class="visually-hidden" data-live aria-live="polite"></p>

		<p class="demo-caption" data-caption></p>

		<div class="demo-why">
			<h2>Why this matters</h2>
			<p>
				The luminosity function is the census: how many galaxies there are of each
				brightness, in a given volume. Run it at several epochs and you can watch
				the population build up. It is one of the sharpest tests a model of galaxy
				formation has to pass, which is why the GALFORM prediction is drawn here
				next to the measurement rather than somewhere else.
			</p>
			<p>
				The catch is that PAU measures distances from photometry, not spectra. Most
				of those redshifts are good; a few are badly wrong. Switch between the two
				photo-<i>z</i> models and watch the bright end: the Gaussian assumption keeps
				the lightcone close to its own truth, while the realistic model, which has
				outliers, scatters faint nearby galaxies into the luminous bins and lifts
				the bright end well above it. That excess is not galaxies. It is mistakes.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="demo-deep">
			<h2>The detail</h2>

			<p>
				Measured with the 1/V<sub>max</sub> estimator on an <i>i</i>-band selected
				sample, <i>i</i><sub>AB</sub> &le; 23, over 31 deg<sup>2</sup> in the PAUS
				W1 and W3 fields. Absolute magnitudes are <i>M</i> &minus; 5&thinsp;log<sub
					>10</sub
				>&thinsp;<i>h</i>, from per-band random-forest k-corrections trained on the
				GALFORM lightcone. W1 and W3 are shown separately rather than combined, so
				that the scatter between two independent patches of sky stays visible.
			</p>

			<p>
				Error bars are the total 1&sigma;: a Monte-Carlo over flux errors and
				photo-<i>z</i> (<span data-fill="mc">500</span> realisations) added in
				quadrature to a delete-one jackknife over
				<span data-fill="jk">64</span> equal-area regions, which is what carries the
				uncertainty from large-scale structure. Shaded bands are the 16th to 84th
				percentile of the lightcone under each photo-<i>z</i> model.
			</p>

			<p>
				The dashed line is the completeness limit, and it is a property of the
				survey rather than of the galaxies. Because the sample is selected on
				observed <i>i</i>-band magnitude, at each redshift there is an absolute
				magnitude below which PAUS stops seeing galaxies at all. Fainter than that
				line the measurement is a lower limit, not a census. It is set where the
				<i>i</i>&thinsp;&le;&thinsp;23 selected lightcone first drops below 90 per
				cent of the full sample.
			</p>

			<p>
				Method, tables and figures follow <a
					href="https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stag362"
					rel="noopener"
					target="_blank">Koonkor et al. (2026)</a
				>, <i>MNRAS</i> <strong>547</strong>(3), 1&ndash;24. The numbers plotted here
				are the paper's own tables, repackaged for the browser; the paper is the
				authoritative version.
			</p>

			<div class="demo-downloads">
				<a href="/data/luminosity-function/README.md">Data notes</a>
				<a href="/data/luminosity-function/lf.json">lf.json</a>
				<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.16042" rel="noopener" target="_blank">arXiv:2511.16042</a>
				<a
					href="https://github.com/SKoonkor/skoonkor.github.io/blob/main/tools/build_lf_data.py"
					rel="noopener"
					target="_blank">build script</a
				>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>

	<noscript>
		<p>
			This demo plots the luminosity function in your browser, so it needs JavaScript.
			The figure from the paper is on the <a class="cactus-link" href="/research/paus-luminosity-function/">research page</a>.
		</p>
	</noscript>
</PageLayout>

<script>
	import { init } from "@/scripts/explore/luminosity-function.js";

	const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-demo]");
	if (root) void init(root);
</script>
